#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096329 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "6010c0f3-67d3-5b21-af26-695a864fb42a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3541" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\sin{x}-2\\cos{x}$ can be written as $R\\sin(x-\\phi)$ with $0^\\circ  <       \\phi <        90^\\circ$ and $R>0,$ what is the value of $\\phi?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = R\\sin(x-\\phi),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x   = (R \\cos \\phi ) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi ) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096330 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "91e6008e-5c37-55aa-8cf6-cac61198f459" :question/math-academy-id "e-3542" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x$ can be written as $R\\sin(x+\\phi)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x   = R\\sin(x+\\phi),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x    = (R \\cos \\phi ) \\sin x + (R \\sin \\phi ) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={\\sqrt{2}}^{2}+{\\sqrt{7}}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =2+7 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =9 \\\\ R & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096331 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "557b1b6e-c54a-5fa9-85e1-3cea346c3bd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3543" :question/problem "Write $3\\sin x-2\\cos x$ in the form $R\\sin(x-\\phi)$ with $\\phi \\in \\left( 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$. Round $\\phi$ to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x    = R\\sin(x-\\phi) ,\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x   = (R \\cos \\phi ) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi )\\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.588\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(3)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+4 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =13 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\sin(x)-2\\cos(x) \\approx \\sqrt{13} \\sin \\left(x - 0.588 \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096332 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/id #uuid "9ea36397-ab9d-5567-8b38-3e83ba624e7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3544" :question/problem "Write $3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x$ in the form $R\\cos(x+\\phi)$ with $\\phi \\in \\left( 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x   = R\\cos(x+\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x   = (R \\cos \\phi) \\cos x -( R \\sin \\phi )\\sin x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =12 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\left(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aebfd566-b5db-5823-938c-2a630ec41af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299573} {:answer/id #uuid "559c1683-251a-5fee-bd02-09c7316f6e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299574} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5d1eec-370e-53ce-a514-be7c682b5e13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299575} {:answer/id #uuid "25d7517b-02d7-5a40-8537-b21c82c16332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299576} {:answer/id #uuid "d67ef0c7-cfb8-5e2e-a2e5-22b6bcc4c798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25d7517b-02d7-5a40-8537-b21c82c16332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299576} :answer-field/id #uuid "be30ef54-6a6a-556f-bd7c-c4ae25a85734" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15713217-5047-5f4b-8ba4-2a3e36222762" :question/math-academy-id "q-107555" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+4 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =9 \\\\ R & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21967a74-a70c-5d80-9061-e2f8238e3b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266559} {:answer/id #uuid "ce34e396-33a4-584c-9f1d-8f7056a38a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266560} {:answer/id #uuid "623ea025-1e92-59f8-926d-0d25222e7e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266561} {:answer/id #uuid "ec117651-6ebe-569e-b39f-3266d56d5381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266562} {:answer/id #uuid "7a963b7b-2737-594d-9e91-6c643c1b46ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce34e396-33a4-584c-9f1d-8f7056a38a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266560} :answer-field/id #uuid "c858464a-8f09-513f-bda6-85d1356abf8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3afc8de-0496-5d66-b501-f8daf7600278" :question/math-academy-id "q-107565" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c51c9d3f-6e82-56cf-a49b-fb5434df1b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299580} {:answer/id #uuid "0968df96-9af7-5455-8c52-0851f7b8e7e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299581} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc000fb-7997-5b05-8bc0-66fee55902fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299582} {:answer/id #uuid "91fed485-ac85-58d5-a02b-8198a1260444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299583} {:answer/id #uuid "907fb84b-2bc2-5530-b0a4-2e17b6287e30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91fed485-ac85-58d5-a02b-8198a1260444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299583} :answer-field/id #uuid "1594ce40-395f-5cb0-98ed-bd958a269d1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50a9ebe0-71cc-5992-ae58-512f2d215118" :question/math-academy-id "q-107566" :question/problem "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf8168b0-0a26-5616-9a54-aeb2ba97df5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299587} {:answer/id #uuid "b50dc54e-5696-52e0-aecb-45bda389ceff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299588} {:answer/id #uuid "8d18f2b5-ac62-53dc-8399-cf003c234260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299589} {:answer/id #uuid "2688dc9d-f91e-5b09-9089-45ba7755bfdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299590} {:answer/id #uuid "3990a6ad-690b-54b4-a49e-18a9769bab10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b50dc54e-5696-52e0-aecb-45bda389ceff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299588} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6e0514f-735b-5234-886e-bba0f0fab573" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe246caf-ce2b-52bd-a786-74626585ee11" :question/math-academy-id "q-107571" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{6} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\ ϕ & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{6})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =6+2 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =8 \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "959266a1-b43b-5ccf-85f5-33feb2ee6293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266573} {:answer/id #uuid "ca59d549-212d-5afa-a3a0-be266414dd28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266574} {:answer/id #uuid "3415ec0b-63df-5359-ada7-30638d1b4f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+0.464)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266575} {:answer/id #uuid "10598812-0f6a-5d50-bf5a-847740aef2d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.351)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266576} {:answer/id #uuid "b96f504f-5d44-5838-a29e-902c6f03b066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.150)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca59d549-212d-5afa-a3a0-be266414dd28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266574} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8723bed-db8d-58c9-bc0f-1d4124803bc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db0aa66b-7e8e-5027-b840-3d5fc4d9828f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107577" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)≈$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =2 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2) \\\\  & =1.107\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(2\\sqrt{5})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+4\\cdot 5 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)≈5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b37b80c-7523-5b79-a685-2b5230d8378c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{20}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.896)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299594} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0067ac-71c0-55b1-9c88-3be58b65cea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{40}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.968)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299595} {:answer/id #uuid "b68690ab-39b4-5ec0-a87b-a3c61e9381a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.103)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299596} {:answer/id #uuid "23d3f2a2-6175-5e6b-98fb-3dee85b7592c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.689)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299597} {:answer/id #uuid "4e20f637-fb04-5811-a251-bf2a46b8c580" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.896)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e20f637-fb04-5811-a251-bf2a46b8c580" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.896)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299598} :answer-field/id #uuid "a30d2232-7c02-5757-91ca-f86a0dcf8ab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db5a83a4-ad24-5ad1-a438-4f752f0186b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-11291" :question/problem "$4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & ≈0.896\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{R}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ & ={5}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =25+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =41 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{41}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$ \n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈\\sqrt{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.896)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "895a52d3-901d-5a3d-bcf3-e59e1972e8aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093706} {:answer/id #uuid "ab538622-6dee-5a95-8eff-75ccb9d525cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093707} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc19cb4-22f9-59f6-b0cf-3bd2143495fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093708} {:answer/id #uuid "7786a5b9-7afc-5184-a6ed-3163876fc05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093709} {:answer/id #uuid "77b87860-d1c7-5c53-92fd-e7f7daaf0d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab538622-6dee-5a95-8eff-75ccb9d525cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093707} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b9b2d63-d5fe-5355-b91d-cce5cca7c1c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60acf08a-2dba-5856-a17a-f9ec2a8623fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-11296" :question/problem "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b7b17ec-8aaa-5772-a0fc-5d2c5bee28d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266597} {:answer/id #uuid "205445fb-23cb-5ea2-ba6a-d6bbde11cc46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b7b17ec-8aaa-5772-a0fc-5d2c5bee28d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266597} :answer-field/id #uuid "04c55e61-c577-55c9-90b3-2c61887c4e82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cec72bc3-9ce9-56a6-be97-f8888cf3eb33" :question/math-academy-id "q-274609" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $0<ϕ<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $R>0,$ what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for cosine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9dd3d0-35bf-5aa8-80c2-d3af0387b25c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{15}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299602} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0e9e15-d6aa-5793-b29c-a81a96fb60c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299603} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7e98c2-c588-5ccf-93fc-57ee0075732c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{35}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299604} {:answer/id #uuid "bf539e6c-9683-5970-a6b8-76bd7812b4ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299605} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbe4b47-3978-5026-a327-9e5f70f75379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf539e6c-9683-5970-a6b8-76bd7812b4ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299605} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa78be67-d613-58c8-97cf-414b4c45494e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4211d938-5e0f-5a8f-bb30-ab83971bd4ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-30578" :question/problem "$-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & ={4}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =32 \\\\ R & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{45}^{∘})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abdba17c-ddca-5813-8832-901ff88a1adf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos (x - \\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095173} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d673d2-953b-5915-b717-3f11104a41ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\cos (x - \\frac{π}{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095174} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3d718c-4e0f-5d1e-bbd8-a9618cfe962e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos (x - \\frac{π}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095175} {:answer/id #uuid "4da046b0-6db0-5896-8e56-abfc95c537b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cos (x - \\frac{π}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095176} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf2366f-8017-58c1-b288-50532ba2f0c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos (x - \\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe3d718c-4e0f-5d1e-bbd8-a9618cfe962e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos (x - \\frac{π}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095175} :answer-field/id #uuid "68c19438-0750-5202-8c3d-71bea293c478" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d31aaa3c-4e44-57d5-8b17-880f83f68511" :question/math-academy-id "q-30580" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =1+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =4 \\\\ R & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "055b7f47-2047-59ac-ab6b-db10bd1d99bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266604} {:answer/id #uuid "1b05f1ba-cbdb-5c22-ae61-26fd8698cfd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266605} {:answer/id #uuid "7d80862d-ceed-5218-a10a-5fa7310fcca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266606} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae4900d-1807-5425-9844-93cf3e86d8cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266607} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9b90bb-0591-5edb-8707-f322beaccd3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b05f1ba-cbdb-5c22-ae61-26fd8698cfd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266605} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fa1131c-da92-5cd8-8b0a-6ac1d605eea1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fd10193-69cb-5212-ac5c-9e4d5831b0e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-30588" :question/problem "$\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =3 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74765624-2997-556c-bb1d-96da05bb7da0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266611} {:answer/id #uuid "b22eb970-03fb-5ee4-b8e1-f0ace298ff5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74765624-2997-556c-bb1d-96da05bb7da0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266611} :answer-field/id #uuid "1003564f-4545-5b97-90dc-4d5adaced41e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50f75dc0-69cb-5287-84d4-979b01969621" :question/math-academy-id "q-327669" :question/problem "Given that the expression $7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=7 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={7}^{2}+(6\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =49+72 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =121 \\\\ R & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cef0c156-0af1-5a35-afe8-010565294c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299609} {:answer/id #uuid "16ff5ae1-64d8-55a5-97fa-5ea6ed04364c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16ff5ae1-64d8-55a5-97fa-5ea6ed04364c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299610} :answer-field/id #uuid "f33140b1-86d5-5461-9ec1-b721fea76069" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5147ca83-8a3d-5ecb-8710-282192ad5618" :question/math-academy-id "q-327702" :question/problem "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ)$ with $R>0$, what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d51fa9-e55e-56a6-b3c7-3bfdcb6468f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266622} {:answer/id #uuid "34cd456b-03d7-5b40-b2d4-4bf35537c125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17d51fa9-e55e-56a6-b3c7-3bfdcb6468f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266622} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4a84f0-1887-5f8a-8f60-ebafa1362b37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4162ecbe-b322-51b0-80d6-aa2e8ed16fcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-327979" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0,$ what is the **exact** value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($ \n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f50bc66-e728-5185-a6ff-e2c78f9cf419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f50bc66-e728-5185-a6ff-e2c78f9cf419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299613} :answer-field/id #uuid "f27cf1fa-5be8-598a-8a55-000615a29bdd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299612} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121f5b62-7509-59b6-844c-10d987d22245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.675" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "121f5b62-7509-59b6-844c-10d987d22245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.675" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299615} :answer-field/id #uuid "58182dcf-2261-57cd-a5cf-e3a3276baf22" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f2fc169-ddc7-5649-97f0-be43b7857d67" :question/math-academy-id "q-327980" :question/problem "Express $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Use the **exact value** for $R,$ and round $ϕ$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n$5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=5 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\ ϕ & ≈0.675\\,\\text{rad},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡(ϕ)+{R}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡(ϕ) & ={4}^{2}+{5}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡(ϕ)+{cos}^{2}⁡(ϕ)) & =16+25 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =41 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{41}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)≈\\sqrt{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.675)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6"